(* ============================================================================
   6. WHAT IS NOT THE WRITER'S JOB. `fmt` does not re-wrap prose or reorder
      attributes. A spelling the AST records is the author's and is reproduced
      (`*` vs `-` bullets, ordered-list dialect, thematic-break marker).
      A spelling it does not record is chosen by the writer canonically.

      FENCE LENGTH IS NOT ONE OF THEM: `code_block` records neither fence
      length nor character (PART 12 §3).

      THE WRITER COMPUTES THE WIDTH IT NEEDS. A fence is widened to clear the
      longest fence-character run in its content and narrowed when nothing
      requires the extra width, and §1 holds through both.

      THE COMBINED FORM IS RECORDED: a `strong` parsed from `bold_italic`
      carries `boldItalic` (PART 12 §3), and the writer reproduces it. `/*x*/` is also the spelling Carve teaches, so normalizing
      it away would rewrite the documented form into one documented nowhere.
      Where a letter or digit stands against its outer `/`, the bare token
      does not parse (PART 3 `bold_italic`), so the writer spells the strong
      and its emphasis in the forced form: `a{*/y/*}b` (carve#3217).

      THE THEMATIC BREAK JOINS IT ON THE SAME TERMS. `---`, `***` and `___` are
      three spellings of one construct, and the AST RECORDS the character --
      `marker` (PART 12 §3) -- so the writer reproduces it. THE MARKER IS THE
      CHARACTER, NOT THE RUN LENGTH: `***` and `*****` are ONE spelling here
      and both come back as `***`. The break's run length is not recorded.

      EXCEPT WHERE `---` WOULD OPEN FRONTMATTER. A break with marker `-`, or
      no marker, would be written `---`. At byte 0 of an emitted document,
      with a later `---` line that would close frontmatter, the writer spells
      that first break `***` instead (CARVE-P11-002). Two marker-less breaks
      are an example: writing both as `---` would create frontmatter.

      THE CONDITION IS THE WHOLE EMITTED DOCUMENT, NOT ITS FIRST LINE. An
      opener needs a closer, so three hyphens at byte 0 open nothing unless a
      later line closes them. A lone `---` is written `---`. What an implementation owes
      is CARVE-P11-002's test: read the emitted bytes with its own parser.

      WHICH break moves is not pinned, because more than one departure
      restores §1 and CARVE-P11-002 already says to take the smallest. Usually it is the
      break at byte 0; where the writer itself PROMOTED something else there,
      the departure available is to respell the CLOSER instead. What IS pinned
      is the spelling a moved break takes: `***`.

      AN ABSENT MARKER IS `---`, which is what a tree assembled by hand or by a
      converter carries.
      CARVE-P11-002 still outranks this: a `---` the writer emits at byte 0 can gain a
      frontmatter closer from a later break, and respelling every break in the
      document is then the smallest departure that restores the invariant.

   6b. A FRONTMATTER OPENER IS WRITTEN `---yaml` -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P11-011]. The canonical
      writer spells the format token on the opening delimiter for EVERY
      format, the default one included. Frontmatter in `yaml` comes back as
      `---yaml`, never as a bare `---`; frontmatter in `toml` comes back as
      `---toml`; nothing about the writer's output turns on which format the
      document named.

      `---` and `---yaml` parse identically; the AST records no difference.
      PART 1 ALREADY NAMES THE FORM: `---yaml` is the canonical whole opener.
      A READER'S LENIENCY IS NOT A WRITER'S LICENSE: accepting bare `---`
      as yaml does not change the writer's required spelling.

   6c. AN EMPTY-STRING ATTRIBUTE IS WRITTEN AS A BOOLEAN -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P11-012]. An
      attribute whose AST value is the empty string comes back as the bare name:
      `[x]{kbd}`, not `[x]{kbd=""}`. `boolean_attribute` is the production the
      language has for "this attribute is present and has an empty value"
      (PART 4), so that is the form the writer emits. `{kbd}` and `{kbd=""}`
      therefore parse to the same value; their authored distinction is not in
      the ordinary AST and the writer uses the canonical bare spelling.

      EXCEPT WHERE THE NAME STARTS WITH `_`, which `boolean_attribute` refuses.
      `_x_=""` stays `{_x_=""}`: emitting `{_x_}` would write a
      forced underline, and `{_u}` would write text. The rule is "use the
      dedicated production where the production applies", and here it does not.

      THE WRITER USES THE DEDICATED PRODUCTION, which is the one rule behind
      all three of these:

        `{lang=fr}`   ->  `{:fr}`     `language_attribute`
        `{lang=""}`   ->  `{:}`       the same, empty
        `{kbd=""}`    ->  `{kbd}`     `boolean_attribute`

      and `lang` keeps the sigil when it is written bare, so `{lang}` is `{:}`
      rather than `{lang}` -- the more specific production wins where both
      apply.

      THIS IS NOT CARVE-P11-011'S MIRROR: unlike bare frontmatter `---`, the grammar
      has a dedicated production for an empty-string boolean value.
      A COMPACT SEMANTIC SPAN IS WHERE IT SHOWS: `[Tab]{kbd}` is
      CARVE-P10-003's form; its value is discarded.

   6d. A CODE FENCE OPENER IS WRITTEN GLUED TO ITS INFO STRING -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P11-013].
      The canonical writer emits NO padding space between the fence run and
      the info string: ```php, never ``` php. The two separator slots INSIDE
      the info string take exactly one space each, so a fence carrying a
      header and a label comes back as

          ```php "src/Auth.php" [Composer]

      whatever run of spaces the author wrote in either slot. That pins the
      whole opener LINE rather than its first token, and it holds for every
      info string, not only a language: a header or a label with no language
      ahead of it is glued the same way (```"src/app.js"), and so is the raw
      passthrough selector (```=html, PART 9 §20).

      PART 2 ALREADY NAMES THE FORM: its `fenced_code_block` production
      calls the glued spelling canonical, and a reader accepts either spacing
      there.
      THE TREE RECORDS NO SLOT: `code_block` records no padding, fence length
      or fence character (PART 12 §3), so the writer chooses the canonical form.

   6e. A TABLE CELL'S CONTENT IS PADDED -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P11-014].
      The canonical writer emits every cell as its PREFIX glued to the opening
      pipe, then ONE space, then the content, then one space before the closing
      pipe. The prefix is the kind marker `=`, the alignment marker and the
      attribute block, in the order PART 9 §5 T10 fixes:

          |= Heading |
          |={.total} Total |= 99 |
          |=<{.h} Name |=>{.c} Score |{.head}

      An EMPTY cell takes a single space (`|= |`, `| |`), not one from each end,
      so a column does not grow a space each time the document is formatted.

      A ROW OF EMPTY CELLS IS WRITTEN AS A ROW. Every AST cell gets one
      explicit slot, so one empty data cell is `| |` and two are `| | |`; a
      marked empty cell keeps its prefix and the space after it (`|=~{.x} |`).
      PART 9 §5 CARVE-P9-082 reads that row back with the same row count,
      cell count, kinds, alignment, attributes and empty children. A table
      with zero rows has no spelling, and this clause does not give it one.

      THE PREFIX IS GLUED BECAUSE THE READER REQUIRES IT. A space in front of
      `=` or of an attribute block makes it literal cell content (PART 9 §5
      T10, corpus `table cell attributes`), so only the CONTENT side is the
      writer's to choose. An UNPREFIXED cell was always padded (`| a | b |`);
      prefixed and unprefixed cells therefore share one padding rule, and the
      padded side is the one a reader can scan.

      THE TREE RECORDS NO SLOT, the same reason CARVE-P11-013 gives: `table_cell` records
      its padding no more than `code_block` records the space before its info
      string (PART 12 §3), so the writer has nothing to reproduce and must
      CHOOSE.

   6e1. EMPTY DESTINATIONS HAVE A CANONICAL SPELLING -- NORMATIVE
      [CARVE-P11-070]. A native inline link with `href: ""` and no reference
      fields writes `[text]()`, or `[]()` when it has no children. A title
      writes `[text]( "title")`; an image with `src: ""` uses the same
      destination slot after `![alt]`. The ordinary attribute run follows the
      close with its existing ownership. The Carve writer inserts no placeholder
      URL or synthetic child. A metadata-free empty
      `link_reference_definition` writes `[label]:`, preserving its hoisted
      node and the `ref`/`rawRef` fields of uses that resolve through it.

      The Markdown target uses angle brackets for an empty destination:
      `[text](<>)`, `[text](<> "title")`, or the corresponding image form.
      When it preserves an empty reference definition, it writes
      `[label]: <>`. These forms read back with an empty destination in
      CommonMark.

      A supplied empty-definition node with a title or attributes has no
      native spelling for those fields. A checked writer reports
      `field-unspellable` for each discarded field under CARVE-P11-046.
      Importing a foreign reference use as a titled inline link likewise
      reports any definition node or metadata it discards; a successful
      visible link is not evidence of complete AST preservation. Canonical
      writing and a second `fmt` pass preserve all spellable fields.


   6e2. OPTIONAL TABLE ALIGNMENT -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P11-071].
      An implementation MAY offer an `alignTables` formatting mode. It is
      opt-in; without it, CARVE-P11-014 remains the canonical table spelling.
      An implementation that offers the mode MUST use the following spelling.
      An implementation without the mode MUST reject a request for it rather
      than silently writing the canonical spelling.

      For each physical row line, form each cell segment from the glued prefix
      of CARVE-P11-014, one space, its content as the canonical writer spells
      it on that line and one trailing space. An empty cell has only the space
      after its prefix. Measure the written source segment, including escapes,
      inline markers and entity spellings. Measure the
      display width of each segment separately. The width of a grid column is
      the greatest segment width in that column across every physical line of
      the table. Add spaces after the segment and before its closing pipe until
      it reaches that width. Do not add padding before content, even for right
      or center aligned cells. A span marker `<` or `^` occupies its own source
      cell and contributes to that grid column's width; it does not divide its
      width among columns.

      A separator line (CARVE-P11-066) has no cell content segment. Measure each
      separator segment by its authored dash run and alignment colons. It
      contributes to the column width. Add only trailing spaces to reach that
      width: do not change its dash count or colon placement, which the tree
      records. An extra separator entry beyond the last header cell contributes
      to its own grid column. The same rule applies when a delimiter separator
      is required to preserve a header containing span markers.

      A `+` continuation line participates as a physical row line. Preserve
      the line split and position recorded by `cell_wrap.row`, `.line`, `.col`
      and `cellWraps[].offset` (PART 12 CARVE-P12-069); measure and pad its
      cells by the same rule as
      ordinary row lines. The leading `+` replaces the leading pipe and is
      outside the first cell segment. A row attribute block following the last
      closing pipe, a caption and material outside the table do not enter any
      column width.

      Display width uses Unicode 18.0.0 extended grapheme clusters (UAX #29).
      A cluster has width two if any code point in it has East_Asian_Width W or
      F in Unicode 18.0.0 (UAX #11); every other cluster has width one,
      including ambiguous-width A. Spaces added for padding have width one.
      Implementations MUST use these pinned property values regardless of the
      host runtime's Unicode version.

      Alignment changes only spelling: parsing an aligned table yields the
      same tree as parsing its canonical form. Formatting an aligned table
      again with `alignTables` yields identical bytes. A formatter's `--check`
      operation with this mode compares the source against aligned output.
      The byte fixtures are in `resources/spec/table-alignment-fixtures.json`.

   6f. PADDING IS NOT AN ESCAPE WHERE THE PRODUCTION ADMITS PADDING --
      NORMATIVE [CARVE-P11-015]. CARVE-P11-014's padding removes the need for
      `<`/`>`/`~`/`{` guards because its three prefix slots are read GLUED to
      the opening pipe, so one space in front of the content puts it out of
      their reach. That argument holds only where the construct's OWN production
      forbids the padding, and one class of cell construct is written WITH the
      padding inside it:

        span_cell      = rowspan_marker | colspan_marker ;
        rowspan_marker = {space}, '^', {space} ;
        colspan_marker = {space}, '<', {space} ;

      A cell whose whole payload is `^` or `<` therefore re-reads as a SPAN
      however it is padded, and CARVE-P11-014's space does nothing for it. §2 is what
      applies, by its own test: omitting the escape changes the re-parsed AST,
      so the payload is escaped and the writer emits `| \^ |` and `| \< |`.

      THE RULE IS THE TEST, NOT THE TWO CHARACTERS. A cell payload is escaped
      wherever omitting the escape would make the cell re-read as something
      other than content, which is §2 run over the CELL rather than over the
      line. Naming `^` and `<` here would be an enumeration that goes stale the
      next time a cell-level marker is added.

   6g. A TASK MARKER IS WRITTEN WITH THE STATE THE AUTHOR CHOSE -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P11-041].
      PART 2 admits nine task marker spellings while rendering collapses
      them to two. The AST records the authored character (PART 12,
      `list_item.taskState`) and the writer emits it.

      THE FIELD IS THE SPELLING, `checked` IS THE MEANING. This is §6's own
      rule, not an exception to it: a spelling is the author's to keep BECAUSE
      THE AST RECORDS IT, and the state is recorded exactly as `bulletChar`
      records `*`. An absent field is the default for the box -- `x` when
      `checked` is true, ` ` when it is false -- which is what a tree assembled
      by hand or by a converter carries.

      `X` IS A CASE, NOT A STATE. `[X]` is written `[x]`.
      The field admits eight characters rather than nine for that reason: the
      two spell one state, nothing downstream can tell them apart, and §1 is
      satisfied because the AST they parse to is the same one.

      `[_]` SPELLS THE OPEN STATE. A writer retains `_` when it appears in
      `taskState`; absent `taskState` still writes `[ ]` for an open item.

      Every state but `x`/`X` renders an unchecked box (PART 2 `task_state`).
      The authored character also survives a format cycle.

   6h. A TABLE'S SEPARATOR ROW IS WRITTEN WHERE THE AUTHOR WROTE ONE -- NORMATIVE
      [CARVE-P11-066]. A header row has two spellings, `|= cell |` and a plain
      row above a `|---|` separator, and they parse to the same rows and the
      same alignments. The AST records which one the author wrote, in
      `table.separatorWidths` (PART 12 §3), and the writer reproduces it: with
      the field, a separator row; without it, `|=` header cells.

      A SEPARATOR FORM REQUIRES AN ALL-HEADER FIRST ROW. An interchange
      tree may carry the field beside a first row containing non-header cells,
      but a separator would promote those cells under PART 9 T7.1. The source
      writer does not change a cell's meaning to preserve the field's spelling.
      A source-conversion API that refuses unspellable fields refuses this
      combination with its typed source-unspellable error. A producer providing
      a lossy fallback keeps the native form and omits `separatorWidths`,
      reporting `field-unspellable`, node `table`, field `separatorWidths`,
      through the conversion-diagnostics channel of CARVE-P11-046 [CARVE-P11-046].

      ALIGNMENT IS WRITTEN ONCE. In a separator form, a header cell with a
      separator entry uses that entry's colon spelling and carries no native
      alignment marker. A header cell past the last entry keeps its native
      alignment marker, having no separator slot. No column is given both a
      native marker and a separator colon.

      THE FIELD IS THE SPELLING, THE ROWS ARE THE MEANING. This is §6's rule
      again, on the same terms as `bulletChar` and `thematic_break.marker`: a
      spelling is the author's to keep BECAUSE THE AST RECORDS IT.

      THE WIDTHS ARE WRITTEN AS RECORDED, one entry per separator cell, each a
      run of that many `-` characters. An alignment colon is NOT one of them:
      it is written from the cell's own alignment, `:` leading for left, `:`
      trailing for right, both for center, and `[5, 3]` on a left-and-right
      table comes back as `|:-----|---:|`. On an unvalidated in-memory tree,
      a width below 1 is written as 1, since a separator cell needs a character.

      THE FIELD DECIDES THE CELL COUNT, AND THE WRITER NEITHER PADS TO NOR
      TRUNCATES AT THE HEADER'S WIDTH. The count is the number of ENTRIES, and
      the header row does not enter it. A separator cell the author did not
      write is a cell, and one the author wrote and the writer dropped is a
      cell lost, and §1 breaks either way: the document re-parses with a
      `separatorWidths` the source never had.

      AN ENTRY PAST THE HEADER'S LAST CELL TAKES NO ALIGNMENT, having no cell
      to take one from: `[3, 3]` over a one-cell centered header writes
      `|:---:|---|`.

      IT IS NOT A LAYOUT INSTRUCTION. The widths are the dash runs the author
      typed, not column widths, and nothing downstream aligns a table to them.
      CARVE-P11-014's cell padding is unchanged by this clause.

   7. NO WHITESPACE-ONLY LINE -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P11-016]. `fmt` never emits a line whose only
      content is ASCII spaces or tabs. Such a line is emitted EMPTY.

      EXCEPT where those spaces are VERBATIM CONTENT. Inside a code block or a
      raw block the lines are data, and a line of three spaces renders as three
      spaces:

        ```           ->  <pre><code>a
        a                 [three spaces]
        [3 spaces]        b
        b                 </code></pre>
        ```

      so emptying it would change the document. The rule there applies to the
      STRUCTURAL INDENT only: a verbatim line sitting inside a list item carries
      the item's content-column indent, and when the verbatim content on that
      line is EMPTY the indent alone is what remains -- that is layout, and it is
      omitted. When the verbatim content is itself whitespace, indent and content
      are both reproduced, and the resulting line is whitespace-only by
      necessity.

      THE PARSER KEEPS THAT CONTENT. A verbatim line inside a container is what
      is left after the container strips its prefix (PART 9 §24 C5), and a line
      of spaces is no exception: the residue past the content column is the
      line's content, not a blank line's padding. Inside a list item whose
      content column is 2, a code-block line of four spaces is a code line of
      two spaces, as it is inside a block quote after `> `. A line no wider
      than the content column is an empty code line.

      The case this decides is a blank line INSIDE a list item, where the item's
      continuation is indented. Indenting the blank line to the content column
      would keep the block structure visible in the source; the empty line wins
      anyway.

      The empty line is required. A whitespace-only line is not stable: editors
      that strip trailing whitespace on save, `git apply --whitespace=fix`, and
      CI whitespace checks all rewrite it, so a formatter emitting one produces
      output that ordinary tooling changes behind it, and is not idempotent in
      practice whatever §1 says about it in isolation.

      AND IT DOES EXTEND to whitespace at the end of a line that HAS content.

        `a` + SPACE + newline + `b`   renders  `<p>a\nb</p>`
        `a` + newline + `b`           renders  `<p>a\nb</p>`

      The two are the same document. PART 2's NO TRAILING WHITESPACE clause
      drops the run on EVERY content line, including one before a soft break,
      so a writer that strips it changes nothing about
      `to_html(fmt(x)) == to_html(x)`.

      A DELIMITED COMMENT TAKES NO PAD AGAINST A LEADING LINE BREAK. The
      writer separates `{%` from the comment's text with one space
      (CARVE-P9-042), except where the text begins
      with a line break: the break separates them already, and the space
      would leave the opener's line ending in whitespace.

      A trailing NO-BREAK space is a different matter and IS content, not
      layout -- the author wrote it and it renders as `&nbsp;` -- so an
      implementation whose whitespace test treats U+00A0 as whitespace must
      exclude it here.

      THE SAME LINE DECIDES WHAT AN IMPORT KEEPS -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P11-017].
      An HTML importer meeting a BLOCK ELEMENT whose text content is entirely
      whitespace keeps exactly the characters this clause calls CONTENT, and
      builds no node at all where every character it holds is LAYOUT:

        <p>&nbsp;</p>   ->  a paragraph holding U+00A0, written as itself
        <p> </p>        ->  no node, and `element-dropped` reported
        <p>&#9;</p>     ->  no node, and `element-dropped` reported

      THE CLASS, NOT THE ENTITY. What separates the two rows is the same
      two-character `whitespace` terminal named above and nothing else: ASCII
      SPACE and TAB are layout, together with the line terminators an HTML
      parser folds into them, and EVERY other character is content. So NARROW
      NO-BREAK SPACE (U+202F) and IDEOGRAPHIC SPACE (U+3000) are kept exactly
      as U+00A0 is, and an importer that special-cases `&nbsp;` has implemented
      a different rule that happens to agree on one row. A lone content-space
      line parses back as a PARAGRAPH and a lone ASCII-space line is a BLANK
      LINE, which is the whole of why the two rows differ.

      NORMALIZING TO A SPACE IS THE ONE ANSWER FORBIDDEN OUTRIGHT. It keeps a
      node while discarding the single property that distinguishes U+00A0 from
      a space, and the paragraph it leaves is unspellable, so it vanishes the
      moment the writer runs -- the two exits then disagree about the same
      import, and §1 is broken on a document the importer built itself.

      THE DROP IS REPORTED because it is a real loss: an element the input had
      contributes nothing, which is `element-dropped` and needs no new code.
      KEEPING owes nothing and reports nothing, since a kept character survives
      the write intact. `parse(htmlToCarve(h)) == htmlToAst(h)` holds on the
      first row with no special case, and holds on the second only because the
      node was never built.


   7a. AN EMPTY CONTAINER BODY IS A BLANK LINE HERE TOO -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P11-018]. A COLON
      FENCE whose body is empty is written with a BLANK LINE between its
      opener and its closer:

        ::: note          :::             ::: myclass
        [blank line]      [blank line]    [blank line]
        :::               :::             :::

      -- an admonition, a BARE div and a div with a word class alike, and the
      same for a line block (`::: |`) and a local hard-break block
      (`::: \`). THERE IS NO EXCEPTION ON THIS TARGET: the shape does not vary
      by container kind, by type word, or by attribute. A body that renders
      nothing is an empty body here as it is in CARVE-P10-001, so a container
      holding only a comment takes this shape too. A comment-only line in a
      line block has already become the empty verse line defined by PART 9
      §23, so its paragraph remains body content.

      IT REACHES THE CONSTRUCTS THAT HAVE A CLOSER LINE, which is what a colon
      fence has and a block quote does not. An empty block quote is a `>` line
      and the question does not arise; §7's rule governs that line, not this
      one.

   7b. A FOOTNOTE DEFINITION WITH NO BLOCKS IS WRITTEN WITH THE SENTINEL
      `{empty}` -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P11-019]. A `footnote_definition` whose body holds NO
      blocks is written as the marker, its separator space, and the SENTINEL
      ATTRIBUTE BLOCK `{empty}`:

        [^f]: {empty}

      and NOT as `[^f]:`, which is a paragraph (PART 2, MARKER REQUIRES
      CONTENT): the definition and every reference to it would degrade to
      text. This is CARVE-P11-002. The writer takes the smallest departure that
      re-parses to the tree, and the parse rule is unchanged.

      THE SENTINEL MUST BE A VALID ATTRIBUTE BLOCK. `block_attributes`
      requires at least one attribute and has no blessed-empty form at block
      level (PART 4), so `{}` and `{ }` on this line stay CONTENT:

        [^f]: {empty}   body EMPTY                            -- works
        [^f]: {#i}      body EMPTY                            -- works
        [^f]: { }       body holds the literal text `{ }`     -- does NOT
        [^f]: {}        body holds the literal text `{}`      -- does NOT

      THE SENTINEL IS INERT. `{empty}` is a boolean attribute (PART 9 §14),
      and a block-attribute line with no following block inside its own
      container is dropped (PART 9 §15 A4), so it reaches neither the endnote
      `<li>`, nor the next block, nor the next definition. CARVE-P11-040 writes an
      empty description body the same way.

   7c. A LINE BLOCK'S HARD BREAK IS WRITTEN BARE ONLY WHERE THE BARE NEWLINE
      RE-DERIVES IT -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P11-020]. A line block hardens every line boundary of
      its own accord, AT EVERY DEPTH (PART 9 §23, AN INLINE CONSTRUCT IS NOT A
      DEPTH THE RULE STOPS AT), which is the whole of why the writer may
      spell a `hard_break` there as a BARE NEWLINE rather than as the PART 3
      form. The depth half is load-bearing and not decoration: a reading under
      which a boundary inside an emphasis run stays soft takes the permission's
      own premise away, and this writer rule then LOSES the break on `*a\` over
      `b*` while claiming §1 preservation. The permission is not
      general, and it is stated as
      the PROPERTY it rests on rather than as a list of the places it runs out:

        THE WRITER SPELLS A `hard_break` INSIDE A LINE BLOCK AS A BARE
        NEWLINE WHERE, AND ONLY WHERE, RE-READING THAT NEWLINE YIELDS THE
        SAME TREE. EVERYWHERE ELSE IT WRITES THE PART 3 FORM, `\` BEFORE THE
        NEWLINE.

      §7 strips a content line's trailing whitespace because CARVE-P2-025 drops it on parse. The
      parser does NOT discard it when a backslash follows: CARVE-P2-025 makes the
      run before a line-break backslash INTERIOR, and PART 9 §23 gives that
      the visible consequence. So none of this is a new principle, it is §7
      applied where its condition does not hold.

      A BARE NEWLINE RE-DERIVES A `hard_break` AT A BOUNDARY BETWEEN TWO BODY
      LINES, and nowhere else. CARVE-P9-044 hardens the boundary BETWEEN lines, so a
      newline written between two lines comes back as the break it replaced,
      with the lines around it unchanged. That is the ordinary case and most
      of every stanza.

      WHAT THE PROPERTY EXCLUDES -- CONSEQUENCES, NOT THE RULE. A case that is
      not in this list is decided by the property above, not by the absence of
      a bullet:

        - THE LAST BODY LINE, WHATEVER IT ENDS IN. CARVE-P9-044 hardens the boundary
          BETWEEN lines, and the body's end is not one. So a `hard_break`
          there is the AUTHOR'S OWN (PART 3) and nothing else can produce it:
          the newline after it belongs to the closing fence, or to the blank
          line before the next stanza. `a\` and `a  \` on a last body line
          lose the break OUTRIGHT, with no space involved in either -- which
          is why neither of the two cases below, one about an emptiness and
          one about a space, reaches this one.
        - AN EMPTY LINE. A bare newline there is a BLANK LINE, which ends the
          stanza, so one stanza is written back as two.
        - CONTENT ENDING IN A LONE SPACE. The boundary comes back and the
          space does not: PART 2's NO TRAILING WHITESPACE clause takes it. A
          run of TWO OR MORE columns is already NBSP CONTENT (CARVE-P9-044 MEDIAL
          GAPS) and survives on its own, so it needs no backslash INSIDE a
          stanza -- at the stanza's end the entry above governs it instead.

      Every condition is visible in the tree with NO new field: the first is a
      `hard_break` that is the last node of the stanza's own inline sequence,
      the second is two adjacent `hard_break` nodes with no text between them,
      the third is a text node whose value ends in one space.

      A LINE WHOSE LAST NODE IS A COMMENT IS EXEMPT -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P11-021]. `%%` runs to
      the END OF ITS LINE, so a trailing space there is INSIDE the note and
      not content PART 2 is about to take: stripping it leaves the same node,
      and CONTENT ENDING IN A LONE SPACE therefore does not reach the line.
      Protecting it with a backslash does not leave the same node either --
      the block layer claims the whole line before the inline parser sees it,
      so the `\` lands in the comment's own CONTENT and `%%` comes back as
      `%% \`. An EMPTY comment line is where this bites, because the writer
      spells one as the marker plus a separator space and that space is the
      one the case would have protected.

      THE RULE IS KEYED ON THE NODE, NOT ON THE LINE'S POSITION, which is the
      same exemption reaching the first consequence. A body line whose content
      ends in an `inline_comment` carries NO `hard_break` at the body's end,
      so THE LAST BODY LINE has nothing to spell there and nothing is written.

      WHICH LINE IS LAST IS DECIDED BY THE BREAKS, HOWEVER THEY WERE SPELLED.
      A `hard_break` ENDS the body line it stands at the end of, and what
      follows it is the NEXT body line -- including a line that renders
      nothing. In

        ::: |
        a \
        %% c
        :::

      the last body line is the COMMENT, not `a `, so the backslash on `a ` is
      there under CONTENT ENDING IN A LONE SPACE and not under THE LAST BODY
      LINE, and the comment is written back as its own line below it.

      AN EMPTY LINE INSIDE A VERBATIM RUN IS SPELLED `%%`, because there the
      backslash is not a break but content. A run left unclosed on an earlier
      line swallows an emptied verse line as a NEWLINE in its value (CARVE-P9-044), so
      the tree holds no `hard_break` for this clause to spell and no `comment`
      node to put back -- what the writer has is a value containing an empty
      line, and a blank line would end the stanza. An empty verse line has
      exactly ONE spelling that does not: a comment line, which the block
      layer removes before the run exists, so `%%` re-reads to the empty line
      it was written for and to nothing else. The author's own comment TEXT is
      not recoverable here and is not required to be.

      IT REACHES AN INTERIOR LINE, because the run's own CLOSER takes the last
      one. A tree records no closer for a verbatim run (PART 12 §3), so the
      writer CLOSES what the author left open, and inside verse that closer
      occupies a LINE. `%%` is written for every emptied line the run still
      spans and not for the one the closer lands on, source over the form
      the writer emits for it:

        ::: |     ->     ::: |          the run spans past the emptied line
        `                `              to `x`, so `%%` spells it and the
        %%               %%             closer follows
        x                x`
        :::              :::

        ::: |     ->     ::: |          the run spans ONE boundary, so the
        `                `              closer takes the emptied line and
        %%               `              no empty line is left to spell
        :::              :::

      THIS IS CARVE-P11-002, NOT AN EXEMPTION FROM IT. The per-construct spelling emits
      bytes that do not re-parse to the tree they were written from, so §1
      wins and the spelling yields to "another spelling of the SAME
      construct", which for a `hard_break` is its own PART 3 form: unguarded,
      the bare newline destroys a rendered space, splits one stanza into two,
      and drops a trailing `<br>` from a block's last line.

   7d. AN EMPTY DESCRIPTION BODY IS WRITTEN WITH THE SENTINEL `{empty}` --
      NORMATIVE [CARVE-P11-040]. A `definition_description` whose body holds NO
      blocks is written as the marker, its separator space, and the SENTINEL
      ATTRIBUTE BLOCK `{empty}`:

        :: t
        : {empty}

      and NOT as the term alone, which re-parses to a `<dl>` with no `<dd>`.
      The sentinel, the reason it must be a valid attribute block and the
      reason it is inert are CARVE-P11-019's.

      TWO OTHER SPELLINGS REACH AN EMPTY `<dd>` AND NEITHER IS WRITTEN:

        : {empty}   claims no line below it at any column        -- CANONICAL
        : +         claims a following flush-left block          -- not written
        : %%        holds a `comment` child                      -- not written

      `: +` is the first-block form (PART 9 §17 L4): it attaches a following
      column-0 block, so it spells an empty body only when a blank line
      follows. `: %%` renders alike and fails §1: its description carries a
      `comment` child the source does not have.

   7e. A BLOCK THAT OPENS A TIGHT ITEM IS WRITTEN ON THE MARKER LINE -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P11-042].
      Where a tight list item's first block must be followed by a continuation
      marker, the writer puts that block on the marker line and the `+` on the
      next line (`- > p`, then `+`) for a top-level item, never a bare `- +`
      with the block at column 0 below it. For a nested tight item it first
      writes the block at the item's content column with a blank line before
      it, as L2 permits. It MAY write a `+` at the nested item's marker column
      only when that blank-line spelling would change the parsed tree, including
      tightness or block ownership (markup-carve/carve#3421). If no spelling
      round-trips, it reports a writer loss.

   7e2. A FENCE A DEDENT ENDED IN A NESTED CHILD IS WRITTEN CLOSED -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P11-074].
      A fenced code block that ended because a line below its nested list
      item's column closed that item is written with its closer at the
      fence's base column. In a tight item, the item's next block follows
      without a blank line before the nested sub-list or between that closer
      and the next block's first line:

        - outer
          - head
            ```
            a
            ```
          ```

          tail
          ```

   7f. A REGION MARKER KEEPS ITS LINE -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P11-073]. A region
      include selects the physical lines strictly between two marker lines
      (CARVE-P6-001 I1b), so a writer that moves a marker or opens a line
      beside one changes what the include reads, even when the HTML is the
      same. `fmt` MUST NOT change what a region selects.

      A REGION MARKER, for this clause, is a line `comment` (not a `%%%` fence
      and not the delimited `{% %}` form) whose content, after any leading
      spaces or tabs, starts with `#region` or `#endregion` and does not go on
      with an ASCII letter, digit or `_`. Every other comment is written as
      before.

      (a) THE BLANK LINE BESIDE A MARKER IS THE AUTHOR'S. Between a region
          marker and the sibling block above or below it, the writer writes a
          blank line where the source had one and none where it had none. It
          reads this from `pos`: the two are ADJACENT when the later block
          starts on the line after the earlier one ends, counting the
          attribute lines the writer itself puts above the later block. The
          earlier block MUST be a paragraph, block image, heading, code block,
          thematic break, comment, table, link reference definition, or a div,
          admonition or block quote written with a closing `:::` fence, since a
          `%%` line written directly below anything else can be read into it.
          Otherwise, and in a tree without positions, the ordinary separator
          applies.

      (b) A MARKER KEPT IN A LIST ITEM KEEPS ITS COLUMN. A region marker that
          is a list item's child, adjacent under (a) to the paragraph or block
          image above it in that item (or to a marker this rule placed), and
          whose recorded start column is at or left of the item's own start
          column, is written at the item's MARKER column, not its content
          column. PART 0 X1 reads it back into the same item: the paragraph's
          claim admits it, and the retention it sets admits the next such
          marker.

      (c) A HOISTED DEFINITION DOES NOT CROSS A MARKER. A link reference or
          footnote definition with a region marker anywhere below its source
          line is not collected to the end of the document (§7). It is written
          where the author wrote it, among its siblings in source order, and
          (a) applies to it like any other block. One with no marker below it
          is collected as before, which moves it past no marker.

      With all three, a region whose content is already canonical comes out of
      `fmt` byte for byte, so the include selects the same bytes before and
      after. `tests/fixtures/region-marker-fmt.json` pins it:

        %% #region requirements
        - PHP 8.3 or later
        - Composer 2.7
        %% #endregion requirements

      is its own canonical form. Without (a) the writer opened a blank line
      under the opener; without (b) it wrote the closer at the item's content
      column, two spaces in. So is

        %% #region links
        [ref]: /u
        %% #endregion links

      which without (c) came out as an empty region with the definition
      written after its closer.

      NOT COVERED. Normalization the writer applies to the content itself
      (an escape, a run of blank lines) changes the selected bytes exactly as
      it changes any other text. A marker retained
      in a nested item returns to that item's marker column, which is not
      necessarily the column it was authored at, and a marker in a footnote
      body keeps the body's column. Neither moves the marker out of its
      container, so neither changes which lines lie between the markers.

   8. THE MARKDOWN TARGET'S ESCAPING -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P11-022]. The Markdown renderer is not
      the canonical writer and does not follow §2. It answers to a Markdown
      reader, and THIS TABLE IS ITS ESCAPING RULE FOR TEXT. Every condition is
      read on the EMITTED LINE, across nodes (CARVE-P11-050). The first column is the
      row's label, or the clause that states the row in full.

        row   character      escaped when                             written
        M1    `\` `` ` ``    always                                   `\` and the
              `~` `]`                                                 character
        M1a   `*`            always                                   `\*`
        M1b   `_`            ADJACENT to an unescaped `_`, or where   `\_`
                             another live `_` in the same inline
                             content could close the emphasis it
                             could open (CARVE-P11-023)
        M1c   `[`            ADJACENT to an unescaped `[`, or inside  `\[`
                             an emitted link label or image
                             description (CARVE-P11-023)
        M1d   `-` `+`        at a paragraph line's content position,  `\-` `\+`
                             before a space or tab
        M1e   `.` `)`        after one to nine ASCII digits at a      `\.` `\)`
                             paragraph line's content position,
                             before a space or tab
        M1f   `<`            before an ASCII letter, `/`, `!` or `?`  `\<`
        M1g   `>`            at a line's content position             `\>`
        M1h   `#`            opening a run of one to six hashes at a  `\#`
                             line's content position, closed by a
                             space, a tab or the line's end; and a
                             trailing run of any length after a
                             space or tab on a line emitted as a
                             heading
        CARVE-P11-051   `&`            where the line after it completes a      `\&`
                             character reference
        CARVE-P11-052   `!`            before a `[` the writer emits as markup  `\!`
        §8g   a line's       a paragraph line that would read as a    `\` on that
              first or       thematic break, a setext underline or    character
              marker         an empty list item (T1 to T3)
              character
        CARVE-P11-054   `|`            in a pipe-table cell                     `\|`
        §8i   `:` `.`        in a URL form GFM would autolink (U1,    `\:` `\.`
                             U2)
        CARVE-P11-060   `@`            in an address GFM would autolink (E1)    `<!---->@`
        M2    the character  M2a: it could be read as markup at the   `\` and the
              of an          position the writer has reached, or it   character
              `escaped_text` is `"`, `'`, `-` or `.`. M2b: a `#`
              node           qualifies only at a line's content
                             position (CARVE-P11-025)
        M3    any other      never                                    bare
              character

      M2 EXISTS BECAUSE AN AUTHORED ESCAPE CARRIES INTENT. `\-\-` was written
      so that a processor with smart punctuation on would not read an en
      dash, and `"` `'` `-` `.` are not Markdown metacharacters, so no M1 row
      covers them. A document that escapes nothing gains no backslashes from
      M2. An implementation whose AST has no `escaped_text` node cannot honor
      M2; that is a defect in the AST, not license to skip the row.

   8a. THE MARKDOWN TARGET'S ESCAPING NARROWS ON THE LINE -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P11-023]. Rows
      M1b to M1h of §8's table are conditional. These terms decide them.

      ADJACENT means the position immediately before or immediately after the
      character, in the line the writer is building, holds that same
      character, put there by the writer as MARKUP and not behind a
      backslash.

      `[` IS ALSO ESCAPED INSIDE AN EMITTED LINK LABEL OR IMAGE DESCRIPTION,
      throughout the label's inline text descendants and the image's alt
      text. Code spans, destinations, titles and raw HTML are unchanged. M1
      escapes every `]`, so a bare `[` there would leave an unmatched opener
      that CommonMark 6.3 reads as the link:

        [a [b] c](/u)      ->  [a \[b\] c](/u)

      `_` TAKES ITS SECOND CONDITION OVER THE INLINE CONTENT IT IS EMITTED IN
      (a paragraph, heading or table cell, across its line breaks), by
      CommonMark 6.2 read for the underscore. A run that flanks on both sides
      can neither open nor close, so `company_id` stays bare while `*x*_y_`
      is written `*x*\_y\_`.

      A PARAGRAPH LINE'S CONTENT POSITION, for M1d, includes up to three
      leading spaces. M1d applies to paragraph text only: a marker the
      renderer emits for an actual list remains live, and punctuation inside
      code remains content.

      THE ASTERISK IS UNCONDITIONAL BECAUSE THIS WRITER EMITS `*` AS ITS OWN
      DELIMITER. Emphasis holding two literal asterisks is written `*\*\**`;
      written `****` it reads back as a thematic break.

      THE TABLE IS THE BOUNDARY, NOT A FLOOR. Each conditional row is an
      if-and-only-if. An engine neither drops an escape a row keeps nor keeps
      one a row drops, narrows no further character and loosens no test.

      M1f `<` IS ESCAPED WHEN THE EMITTED LINE WOULD READ IT AS MARKUP --
          NORMATIVE [CARVE-P11-024]. An opening tag, a closing tag, a comment
          and a processing instruction open raw HTML through a CommonMark
          reader, and those four next-characters are the whole hazard. The
          escape is a BACKSLASH, not an entity, and the closing `>` needs
          none: a tag that cannot open cannot be closed.

            a \<b> c       ->  a &lt;b&gt; c

      M1h `#` IS ESCAPED WHERE THE EMITTED LINE WOULD OPEN OR CLOSE AN ATX
          HEADING -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P11-044]. The content position is the
          one CARVE-P11-025 defines, and the escape goes on the `#` that opens the run:

            a               ->  a
               # heading    ->  \# heading

   ============================================================================ *)
